{
  "dom_id": "step-e529",
  "name": "Example: Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice",
  "problem": "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
